---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../../components/navigation/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { buildLocalePath } from '../../../../i18n/utils';

const locale = 'en-US' as const;
const title = 'How to Calculate a Salary Raise (2026 Guide)';
const description =
  'Learn how to calculate a salary raise step by step: turn a percentage into dollars, work out your new salary, and see what the raise adds to each paycheck.';
const canonical = '/en-us/blog/how-to-calculate-a-salary-raise-2026/';
const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BlogPosting',
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished: '2026-11-08',
  dateModified: '2026-11-08',
  author: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'PayRenTax',
    url: 'https://payrentax.me',
  },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'PayRenTax',
    url: 'https://payrentax.me',
  },
  mainEntityOfPage: {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://payrentax.me/en-us/blog/how-to-calculate-a-salary-raise-2026/',
  },
};

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'How do I calculate a salary raise?',
    a: 'Multiply your current salary by the raise percentage written as a decimal — for example, 5% is 0.05 — then add the result to your current salary. A 5% raise on $60,000 is $60,000 × 0.05 = $3,000, so the new salary is $63,000.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a 5% raise on a $60,000 salary?',
    a: 'A 5% raise on $60,000 adds $3,000 a year, bringing the salary to $63,000. That is $250 more per month, or $115.38 more per biweekly paycheck, before deductions.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I find the percentage of a raise I was offered?',
    a: 'Subtract your old salary from the new salary, divide by the old salary, and multiply by 100. A move from $40,000 to $45,000 is ($45,000 − $40,000) ÷ $40,000 × 100 = 12.5%.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does a raise change my hourly rate?',
    a: 'A $25 per hour wage with a 5% raise becomes $26.25 per hour, which is $54,600 a year on a 40-hour week (2,080 hours).',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a raise increase my take-home pay by the full amount?',
    a: 'No. The raise increases your gross pay by the full amount, but deductions such as taxes are taken from the new, higher gross pay, so your take-home pay rises by less than the raise itself.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout locale={locale} title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
    <script
      type="application/ld+json"
      set:html={JSON.stringify({
        '@context': 'https://schema.org',
        '@type': 'FAQPage',
        mainEntity: faqItems.map((f) => ({
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: f.q,
          acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
        })),
      })}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <main class="page">
    <Breadcrumbs locale={locale} trail={[{ label: 'Blog', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog') }, { label: 'How to Calculate a Salary Raise', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog/how-to-calculate-a-salary-raise-2026') }]} />
    <article class="article">
      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="article-date">November 8, 2026</p>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="article-intro">{description}</p>
      </header>

      <p>
        A raise usually arrives as a percentage — 3%, 5%, maybe more — and the
        number that matters is what it adds to your pay in dollars. This guide
        shows how to turn any raise percentage into a new salary, a monthly
        figure, and a per-paycheck amount, using only simple arithmetic.
      </p>

      <div class="tldr-box">
        <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Raise amount</strong> = current salary × raise percentage
            (as a decimal).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>New salary</strong> = current salary + raise amount.
          </li>
          <li>
            A <strong>5% raise on $60,000</strong> adds $3,000 a year:
            $250 a month, $115.38 per biweekly check.
          </li>
          <li>
            To find the <strong>percentage of an offer</strong>, divide the
            increase by your old salary and multiply by 100.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p><strong>On this page</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#what-is">What Is a Salary Raise?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#formula">How to Calculate a Salary Raise in 3 Steps</a></li>
          <li><a href="#paycheck">What a Raise Adds to Each Paycheck</a></li>
          <li><a href="#percentage">How to Work Out the Percentage of a Raise</a></li>
          <li><a href="#over-time">What Raises Add Up to Over Time</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-is" class="article-section">
        <h2>What Is a Salary Raise?</h2>
        <p>
          A salary raise is an increase in your base pay, usually given as a
          percentage of your current salary. If you earn $60,000 and receive a
          5% raise, your salary rises by $3,000 to $63,000 a year. The
          percentage is the same whatever you earn — but the dollar amount it
          produces is bigger on a bigger salary, which is why the same 5% can
          feel very different from one job to the next.
        </p>
        <p>
          Raises are often tied to a performance review, a promotion, or an
          annual pay cycle, and they usually take effect on a set date. Once
          the new salary starts, every paycheck after that date uses the new
          figure. Our guide on
          <a href="/en-us/blog/how-to-ask-for-a-raise-2026/">how to ask for a
          raise</a> covers the conversation itself; this guide covers the
          math, so you know exactly what any offer is worth before you accept
          it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="formula" class="article-section">
        <h2>How to Calculate a Salary Raise in 3 Steps</h2>
        <p>
          Calculating a raise takes three steps, and you only need your current
          salary and the raise percentage:
        </p>

        <div class="formula-box">
          <p><strong>Raise amount = current salary × raise percentage</strong></p>
          <p><strong>New salary = current salary + raise amount</strong></p>
        </div>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Turn the percentage into a decimal.</strong> Divide it by
            100: 5% becomes 0.05, 3% becomes 0.03, and 2.5% becomes 0.025.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Multiply by your current salary.</strong> That gives the
            raise in dollars per year.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Add it to your current salary.</strong> That is your new
            annual salary.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="example-box">
          <p><strong>Example: $60,000 salary, 5% raise</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Raise amount: $60,000 × 0.05 = <strong>$3,000 per year</strong></li>
            <li>New salary: $60,000 + $3,000 = <strong>$63,000 per year</strong></li>
            <li>Monthly increase: $3,000 ÷ 12 = <strong>$250 per month</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          You can check the result instantly with our
          <a href="/en-us/salary/salary-raise/">salary raise calculator</a>,
          which works out the new salary and the per-paycheck amount for any
          percentage. If you are comparing an hourly wage instead, our
          <a href="/en-us/salary/annual-salary-calculator/">annual salary
          calculator</a> converts it to a yearly figure first.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="paycheck" class="article-section">
        <h2>What a Raise Adds to Each Paycheck</h2>
        <p>
          The annual raise is only part of the picture — what you notice is the
          extra amount on each paycheck. Divide the yearly raise by the number
          of pay periods in the year: 52 for weekly pay, 26 for biweekly pay,
          24 for semimonthly pay, and 12 for monthly pay.
        </p>

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Raise on $60,000</th>
              <th>Per year</th>
              <th>Per month</th>
              <th>Per biweekly check</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>2%</td>
              <td>$1,200</td>
              <td>$100</td>
              <td>$46.15</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>3%</td>
              <td>$1,800</td>
              <td>$150</td>
              <td>$69.23</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>4%</td>
              <td>$2,400</td>
              <td>$200</td>
              <td>$92.31</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>5%</td>
              <td>$3,000</td>
              <td>$250</td>
              <td>$115.38</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>10%</td>
              <td>$6,000</td>
              <td>$500</td>
              <td>$230.77</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          Remember that these are gross figures, before deductions. Your
          take-home pay rises by less than the gross raise, because taxes and
          other deductions are taken from the new, higher pay. To see the
          effect on an actual check, run the new salary through our
          <a href="/en-us/salary/paycheck-calculator/">paycheck calculator</a>
          and compare it with your current check — our guide on
          <a href="/en-us/blog/how-to-calculate-your-paycheck-2026/">how to
          calculate your paycheck</a> explains the steps.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="percentage" class="article-section">
        <h2>How to Work Out the Percentage of a Raise</h2>
        <p>
          Sometimes the offer comes as a dollar figure instead of a percentage
          — a new salary of $45,000, say, up from $40,000. To find the
          percentage, subtract the old salary from the new one, divide by the
          old salary, and multiply by 100:
        </p>

        <div class="formula-box">
          <p><strong>Raise percentage = (new salary − old salary) ÷ old salary × 100</strong></p>
        </div>

        <div class="example-box">
          <p><strong>Example: $40,000 to $45,000</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Increase: $45,000 − $40,000 = <strong>$5,000</strong></li>
            <li>Percentage: $5,000 ÷ $40,000 × 100 = <strong>12.5%</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          The same math works for hourly pay. A wage of $25 per hour with a 5%
          raise becomes $25 × 1.05 = $26.25 per hour. On a 40-hour week (2,080
          hours a year), that is $52,000 a year rising to $54,600 — a $2,600
          annual increase. Our
          <a href="/en-us/salary/salary-to-hourly/">salary to hourly
          calculator</a> converts between the two whenever you need to compare
          an hourly offer with a salary.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="over-time" class="article-section">
        <h2>What Raises Add Up to Over Time</h2>
        <p>
          Raises compound: each new raise applies to a salary that already
          includes the last one. A 3% raise every year for three years on a
          $50,000 salary does not add 9% — it adds a little more, because each
          year's 3% is calculated on a higher base:
        </p>

        <div class="example-box">
          <p><strong>Example: $50,000, 3% raise each year for 3 years</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Year 1: $50,000 × 1.03 = <strong>$51,500</strong></li>
            <li>Year 2: $51,500 × 1.03 = <strong>$53,045</strong></li>
            <li>Year 3: $53,045 × 1.03 = <strong>$54,636.35</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          After three years the salary is $54,636.35 — a total increase of
          $4,636.35, compared with $4,500 if the same 3% had been applied to the
          original $50,000 each time. The gap is small over three years, but
          over a career it is the reason steady raises matter more than any
          single one.
        </p>

        <p>
          When a raise takes effect, check the first paycheck at the new rate:
          divide the new salary by your pay periods (26 for biweekly, 12 for
          monthly) and compare the gross figure with your paystub. If it does
          not match, ask payroll — a clear question with the math attached is
          usually resolved quickly.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="faq" class="article-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        {faqItems.map((item) => (
          <>
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </>
        ))}
      </section>

      <section id="related" class="article-section">
        <h2>Related Articles</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/en-us/blog/how-to-ask-for-a-raise-2026/">How to Ask for a Raise (2026)</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en-us/blog/what-is-an-annual-salary-2026/">What Is an Annual Salary? (2026)</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en-us/blog/how-to-calculate-your-paycheck-2026/">How to Calculate Your Paycheck (2026)</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en-us/blog/salary-to-hourly-conversion-2026/">Salary to Hourly Conversion (2026)</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en-us/blog/take-home-pay-explained-2026/">Take-Home Pay Explained (2026)</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
